  "notes": "West leads the ♦K against 5♠. West cashes 2 diamond tricks and switches to the ♥J won by South. The contract is in serious trouble. West will need a singleton ♠J for the trump suit to be playable for no losers. West's play of the ♥J, apparently denying the ♥Q, gives South hope of not having to lose any heart tricks.\n\nSouth should cross to the ♣A and lead the ♠Q. When East follows low and West produces the ♠J, prospects have improved. The ♠10 is also ducked by East. South is able to remain in the dummy and play a heart to the ♥10.\n\nUnfortunately West produces the ♥Q. Usually one should not mind paying off to a clever falsecard like West's ♥J switch. In this case, South should have worked it out. If East really had the ♥Q, the ♠10 would have been covered by the ♠K. South would have been locked in the closed hand and would have had no way to avoid giving East a heart trick.\n\nSouth should not play East to have misdefended in such an easy situation. When the ♠10 is not covered, West should be played for doubleton ♥QJ.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
